Subject: Keep a template argument's `const` when it resolves to a record

`--use-const-newtype-wrapper` marks a type C++ qualified `const` in its own
right, and it already reaches template arguments: `shared_ptr<const int>` comes
out `shared_ptr<__bindgen_marker_Const<c_int>>`, and so does an argument which
is a pointer. An argument which is a *record* silently loses it, so
`shared_ptr<const Foo>` and `shared_ptr<Foo>` are one type in the output.

The qualifier belongs to the node it is written on. For `Container<const Foo>`
clang gives an argument type which is `const` and refers to `Foo`, so bindgen
builds a `const` `Type` whose kind is `ResolvedTypeRef(Foo)`; `Foo` itself is
not `const`. `TemplateInstantiation::try_to_rust_ty` resolves the argument
`through_type_refs()` before rendering it, which lands on `Foo` and leaves the
qualified node - the only node which knows about the `const` - behind. A
builtin or a pointer has no such node to skip, which is why those two kept it.

Read the qualifier off the argument before resolving and re-apply it after,
which is what the pointer arm of `TryToRustTy for Type` already does with a
pointee's constness a few hundred lines above. Doing it at the one place
template arguments are rendered covers every template, `std::` container and
user-written class alike, rather than special-casing any of them.

Nothing changes unless `--use-const-newtype-wrapper` is on, since
`const_wrapper` is what decides whether to wrap at all.

--- a/codegen/mod.rs
+++ b/codegen/mod.rs
@@ -4990,11 +4990,24 @@
             // the def uses.
             .filter(|&(_, param)| ctx.uses_template_parameter(def.id(), *param))
             .map(|(arg, _)| {
+                // `const` belongs to the node it is written on, and resolving
+                // through a type reference lands on a different node: for
+                // `Container<const T>` where `T` is a record, the argument is a
+                // `const` `ResolvedTypeRef` pointing at the record, and the
+                // record itself is not `const`. So read the qualifier before
+                // resolving, exactly as the pointer arm of `TryToRustTy for
+                // Type` reads a pointee's, and put it back afterwards.
+                let is_const = ctx.resolve_type(*arg).is_const();
                 let arg = arg.into_resolver().through_type_refs().resolve(ctx);
                 let ty = arg
                     .try_to_rust_ty(ctx, &())?
                     .with_implicit_template_params(ctx, arg);
-                Ok(ty)
+                // If resolution stayed where it was, or landed on a node which
+                // is itself `const`, `TryToRustTy for Type` has already
+                // qualified `ty` and doing it again would nest the marker.
+                let is_const = is_const &&
+                    !arg.as_type().is_some_and(|ty| ty.is_const());
+                Ok(helpers::const_wrapper(ty, is_const, ctx))
             })
             .collect::<error::Result<Vec<_>>>()?;
 
